Expose exactly this public API:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct DependencyGraph {
    pub dependencies: Vec<Vec<usize>>,
}

pub fn dependency_order(graph: &DependencyGraph, roots: &[usize]) -> Vec<usize>;
```

Each node is its index in `dependencies`, and its vector lists its direct dependencies in deterministic left-to-right order. Inputs are guaranteed to contain valid indices and form a directed acyclic graph.

Return the reachable nodes in depth-first postorder: recursively visit each node's dependencies from left to right, then emit the node. Process roots from left to right. A node shared by multiple parents or repeated in `roots` must be visited and emitted only once, at its first traversal opportunity. For example, dependencies `[[2], [2], []]` with roots `[0, 1]` produce `[2, 0, 1]`.

The graph and roots are shared immutable inputs and must not be modified. Traversal bookkeeping must be stored separately; do not clone or rewrite the graph. Use only the Rust standard library and do not access the filesystem, network, clock, or randomness.","reference_code":"#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct DependencyGraph {
    pub dependencies: Vec<Vec<usize>>,
}

pub fn dependency_order(graph: &DependencyGraph, roots: &[usize]) -> Vec<usize> {
    fn visit(
        node: usize,
        graph: &DependencyGraph,
        visited: &mut [bool],
        output: &mut Vec<usize>,
    ) {
        if visited[node] {
            return;
        }
        visited[node] = true;

        for &dependency in &graph.dependencies[node] {
            visit(dependency, graph, visited, output);
        }
        output.push(node);
    }

    let mut visited = vec![false; graph.dependencies.len()];
    let mut output = Vec::new();
    for &root in roots {
        visit(root, graph, &mut visited, &mut output);
    }
    output
}
